    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point",
    "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}/2$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
